  "query_preview": "..An upper level ridge will be in place through\nThursday leading to temperatures a few degrees above normal to\naround normal through Friday. Highs will generally be in the low\n80s from Tucson west and 70s east of Tucson. \n\nWell to the west Thursday a cutoff low pinches off from a trough\nover the Gulf of Alaska and moves through Baja and Sonora Friday\ninto Saturday. The trough it separated from opens up and \ntravels through the Great Basin Saturday. The trajectory of the \nsouthern stream low will allow moisture to move into the region \nwith PWATs increasing to around 150-200 percent of normal Friday\nand Saturday. This will lead to rain chances beginning Friday \nafternoon and continuing into Saturday. The best chances Friday \nwill be along the international border since the core of the \ntrough will be in Mexico. Saturday will see storm chances mainly\nfrom Tucson north and eastward as the second trough moves \nthrough to the north of the area. Still some uncertainty with \nthe exact tracks of these lows, so favored rainfall areas could \nchange in the coming days. QPF is relatively low with most areas\nseeing up to 0.25\" with localized higher amounts. NBM 90th \npercentile QPF through Saturday maxes out around 0.9\" along the \ninternational border.\n\nSunday looks like it will be a transition day as shortwave\nriding briefly forms before the next Pacific low moves through\nthe region. NBM PoPs are below 10 percent Sunday. Ensembles \nkeep the upper air pattern progressive bringing another trough \nthrough Arizona early next week. Currently this next system \nlooks to be a little further north than the previous with the \nmain axis swinging through Arizona instead of Sonora. The \nmoisture from this weekend doesn't really leave leading to \nanother round of rain chances late Monday into Tuesday of next \nweek.",
